IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cica-dates`

-- Criminal Injuries Compensation Act 2003 (Western Australia) -- compensation paid by the State to a person injured by an
-- offence, and for the loss of the close relatives of a person killed by one: who may apply and under which section
-- (ss 8, 12-17), close relatives (s 4), how and by when to apply (ss 9, 11), the entitlement ending on death (s 10), what
-- can be compensated (ss 6, 30), the
-- most that can be awarded (ss 31-34), compensation for mental and nervous shock (s 35), what bars an award (ss 36-41),
-- interim payments (s 23), what is taken off the award (ss 20(2)(d), 23(3), 42, 43), when it is paid (s 47), future
-- treatment expenses (s 48), the decision and reasons (ss 26, 27), appeals (s 55), what may have to be paid back
-- (ss 23(4), 68), and the offences an applicant can commit (ss 19(5), 63(5), 64(5), 70, 71).
--
-- SEAT: the victim -- the person injured by an offence -- and, where the victim died, the close relative who suffered loss
-- from the death (the claim is made for that relative by the deceased's personal representative). "I was assaulted. Can I
-- claim? By when? How much at most? Will my insurance payout be taken off?" Every opinion or satisfaction of an assessor
-- or of the District Court is asked as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: the compilation on the Western Australian Legislation website (mrdoc_47649), retrieved 7 September 2026, last
-- amended by the Criminal Law (Mental Impairment) Act 2023 (in operation 1 September 2024), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_47649.txt. No regulation amending the s 31 Table is deposited or noted in the
-- compilation; the Table's last item ($75 000) is used.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: ss 1, 2, 8A (machinery); s 7 (construction); the assessor's procedural powers in ss 18, 19(1)-(4), 20(1),
-- (2)(a)-(c), 21, 22, 24, 25, 62A, 63(1)-(4), (6)-(8), 64(1)-(4) (they affect how and when an application is decided, not
-- what the victim is entitled to; s 20(2)(d) is folded into the amount paid, s 20(3) into the amount the assessor is
-- satisfied is just); s 28 (internal); ss 29, 30 (the assessor's discretion, asked as the amount the assessor is satisfied
-- is just); s 44 (payment to the person who paid expenses); s 45 and Part 6 (recovery from the offender: the offender's
-- matter, not the victim's); ss 46, 54, 69(2) (the Consolidated Account); ss 56-58 (the District Court's and the assessor's
-- powers on appeal and referral); ss 65-67 (immunities and costs); Part 8, ss 72, 73 and Schedules 1 and 2 (machinery and
-- transitional provisions).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the victim's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Criminal Injuries Compensation Act 2003 -- the victim of an offence`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `while a charge for the offence is still undecided, none of sections 12 to 17 yet applies; the route depends on how the charge ends (the CEO may ask for the application to be stayed meanwhile, s 22)`
    `the person charged has been charged again with the offence or tried for it, so section 16 does not apply (s 16(1)(f)); the route depends on how that charge ends`
    `sections 12 to 17 give a right to apply to a person who suffers injury (bodily harm, mental and nervous shock, or pregnancy); this Act does not speak to a person who suffered none`
    `the Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8), so it sets no maximum`
    `section 34 applies to an application made on or after 23 September 2003; for an earlier application this Act does not say what the maximum is for unrelated offences by one offender`
    `section 55(1) gives the applicant an appeal against a decision to make or refuse an award, and as to its amount; this Act does not speak to an appeal against other decisions`
    `section 6(1)(g) lets regulations prescribe further personal items, and the regulations are not deposited`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

-- Dates the Act names.
`22 January 1971` MEANS `the day` 22 1 1971
`1 January 1986` MEANS `the day` 1 1 1986
`1 July 1993` MEANS `the day` 1 7 1993
`23 September 2003` MEANS `the day` 23 9 2003
-- The compilation table: the Act other than ss 1 and 2 came into operation on 1 January 2004 (Gazette 30 Dec 2003 p 5722).
`the day this Act came into operation` MEANS `the day` 1 1 2004
-- The compilation table: the Acts Amendment (Justice) Act 2008 Part 7 (which includes its s 31) came into operation on
-- 31 July 2008 (Gazette 11 Jul 2008 p 3253).
`the day the Acts Amendment (Justice) Act 2008 section 31 commenced` MEANS `the day` 31 7 2008

§§ `s 4 -- am I a close relative of the victim`

-- s 4(1): "a close relative of a victim who dies or is injured as a consequence of the commission of an offence, is a
-- person who, immediately before the offence was committed, was -- (a) a parent, grandparent or stepparent of the victim;
-- or (b) the spouse or a de facto partner of the victim; or (c) a child, grandchild or stepchild of the victim."
-- s 4(2): the presumptions of parentage in the Family Court Act 1997 Part 5 Division 11 Subdivision 3 apply (borrowed;
-- whether I am a parent or child is asked as a fact). "De facto partner" is borrowed from the Interpretation Act 1984.
DECLARE `My relationship to the victim` IS ONE OF
    `parent`
    `grandparent`
    `stepparent`
    `spouse`
    `de facto partner`
    `child`
    `grandchild`
    `stepchild`
    `brother or sister`
    `some other relationship (for example aunt, uncle, niece, nephew, cousin, fiance or friend)`

DECLARE `Am I a close relative` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or stepparent of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(a))`
    `yes: I was the spouse or a de facto partner of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(b))`
    `yes: I was a child, grandchild or stepchild of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(c))`
    `no: the relationship must have existed immediately before the offence was committed (s 4(1))`
    `no: section 4(1) does not list my relationship; brothers, sisters and other relatives are not close relatives`

@export Someone in my family was killed or injured by a crime in Western Australia. Am I their "close relative" under the Criminal Injuries Compensation Act 2003?
GIVEN `my relationship to the victim` IS A `My relationship to the victim` @desc How I am related to the person killed or injured by the offence
      `the relationship existed immediately before the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I already had that relationship with them immediately before the offence was committed (for example already married, already their stepchild)
GIVETH AN `Am I a close relative`
DECIDE `Am I a close relative question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF    `my relationship to the victim` EQUALS `brother or sister`
           OR `my relationship to the victim` EQUALS `some other relationship (for example aunt, uncle, niece, nephew, cousin, fiance or friend)`
            THEN `no: section 4(1) does not list my relationship; brothers, sisters and other relatives are not close relatives`
        IF (NOT `the relationship existed immediately before the offence`)
            THEN `no: the relationship must have existed immediately before the offence was committed (s 4(1))`
        IF    `my relationship to the victim` EQUALS `parent`
           OR `my relationship to the victim` EQUALS `grandparent`
           OR `my relationship to the victim` EQUALS `stepparent`
            THEN `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or stepparent of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(a))`
        IF    `my relationship to the victim` EQUALS `spouse`
           OR `my relationship to the victim` EQUALS `de facto partner`
            THEN `yes: I was the spouse or a de facto partner of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: I was a child, grandchild or stepchild of the victim immediately before the offence (s 4(1)(c))`

§§ `ss 8, 12-17 -- can I apply, and under which section`

DECLARE `I am applying as` IS ONE OF
    `the person injured by the offence (the victim)`
    `the personal representative of a person who died as a consequence of the offence`

-- s 5: "convicted" includes a spent conviction, a finding of guilt with no conviction recorded under the Young Offenders
-- Act 1994 s 55, the repealed dismissals of s 5(1)(c)-(g), and being dealt with by a juvenile justice team (s 5(2)).
DECLARE `What happened in the criminal case` IS ONE OF
    `someone was convicted of the offence (including a spent conviction, a finding of guilt with no conviction recorded, or being dealt with by a juvenile justice team)`
    `the person charged was found not guilty, other than on account of mental impairment`
    `the person charged was found not guilty on account of mental impairment`
    `the person charged was found unfit to stand trial`
    `the charge was withdrawn or discontinued, dismissed without a finding, met with no evidence from the prosecutor, or the person charged died or was otherwise not brought to trial`
    `no one has been charged with the offence`
    `a charge for the offence has been laid but not yet decided`

DECLARE `Section` IS ONE OF
    `section 12 (proved offence)`
    `section 13 (alleged offence: acquittal)`
    `section 14 (alleged offence: acquittal due to mental impairment)`
    `section 15 (alleged offence: accused unfit to stand trial)`
    `section 16 (alleged offence: charge not determined)`
    `section 17 (alleged offence: no person charged)`

DECLARE `Can I apply` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` HAS `under` IS A `Section`
    `yes: as the personal representative I may apply for compensation for the loss the close relatives suffered as a result of the death` HAS `under` IS A `Section`
    `no: this Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8)`
    `no: after an acquittal, an application lies only if the applicant claims the offence was committed by someone other than the person acquitted (s 13(2)(b), (3)(c))`
    `no: the personal representative may apply only if a close relative of the deceased suffered loss as a result of the death`

-- The section the case falls under, once a charge (if any) has ended.
-- s 15(1)(a): only an alleged offence "alleged to have been committed on or after 1 January 1986". An accused found unfit
-- for an earlier offence is "for any other reason ... not brought to trial", and s 15 "does not apply", so s 16(1)(e), (g)
-- take the case (fork register in NOTES.md).
GIVEN `the criminal case` IS A `What happened in the criminal case`, `the date of the offence` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Section`
`the section for` `the criminal case` `the date of the offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the criminal case` EQUALS `someone was convicted of the offence (including a spent conviction, a finding of guilt with no conviction recorded, or being dealt with by a juvenile justice team)`
            THEN `section 12 (proved offence)`
        IF `the criminal case` EQUALS `the person charged was found not guilty, other than on account of mental impairment`
            THEN `section 13 (alleged offence: acquittal)`
        IF `the criminal case` EQUALS `the person charged was found not guilty on account of mental impairment`
            THEN `section 14 (alleged offence: acquittal due to mental impairment)`
        IF    `the criminal case` EQUALS `the person charged was found unfit to stand trial`
          AND `the date of the offence` `falls on or after` `1 January 1986`
            THEN `section 15 (alleged offence: accused unfit to stand trial)`
        IF `the criminal case` EQUALS `no one has been charged with the offence`
            THEN `section 17 (alleged offence: no person charged)`
        OTHERWISE `section 16 (alleged offence: charge not determined)`

-- s 8: "This Act does not apply to or in relation to an offence that was committed before 22 January 1971."
-- s 12(1), 13(2), 14(2), 15(2), 16(2), 17(2): "A person who suffers injury as a consequence of the commission of [the
-- offence] may apply for compensation for the injury and any loss also suffered."
-- s 12(2), 13(3), 14(3), 15(3), 16(3), 17(3): "If a person, being a close relative of a person who dies as a consequence of
-- the commission of [the offence], suffers loss as a result of the death, the personal representative of the deceased may
-- apply for compensation for that loss."
-- s 13(2)(b), (3)(c): the applicant "claims that the alleged offence was committed but by a person other than the
-- acquitted person". s 16(1)(f): "the person charged is not otherwise charged with the alleged offence or tried for it".
-- "injury means bodily harm, mental and nervous shock, or pregnancy" (s 3).
@export I was hurt by a crime in Western Australia, or someone in my family was killed by one. Can a claim be made under the Criminal Injuries Compensation Act 2003, and under which section?
GIVEN `I am applying as` IS AN `I am applying as` @desc Whether I apply as the person injured, or as the executor or administrator (personal representative) of someone who died from the offence, for their close relatives' loss
      `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was committed (ISO 8601)
      `what happened in the criminal case` IS A `What happened in the criminal case` @desc What has happened to any charge for the offence
      `I claim someone other than the acquitted person committed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if the person charged was found not guilty (not on account of mental impairment): whether I claim the offence was committed, but by someone other than the person acquitted
      `the person charged has been charged again or tried for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only if the charge was withdrawn, dismissed without a finding or not brought to trial: whether that person has since been charged again with the offence or tried for it
      `the victim suffered injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injured person suffered bodily harm, mental and nervous shock, or pregnancy as a consequence of the offence
      `a close relative suffered loss from the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where the victim died: whether a close relative (parent, grandparent, stepparent, spouse, de facto partner, child, grandchild or stepchild) suffered loss as a result of the death
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I apply`
DECIDE `Can I apply question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls before` `22 January 1971`
            THEN `answered` `no: this Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8)`
        IF `what happened in the criminal case` EQUALS `a charge for the offence has been laid but not yet decided`
            THEN `not covered` `while a charge for the offence is still undecided, none of sections 12 to 17 yet applies; the route depends on how the charge ends (the CEO may ask for the application to be stayed meanwhile, s 22)`
        IF    `the section` EQUALS `section 13 (alleged offence: acquittal)`
          AND (NOT `I claim someone other than the acquitted person committed it`)
            THEN `answered` `no: after an acquittal, an application lies only if the applicant claims the offence was committed by someone other than the person acquitted (s 13(2)(b), (3)(c))`
        IF    `the section` EQUALS `section 16 (alleged offence: charge not determined)`
          AND `the person charged has been charged again or tried for it`
            THEN `not covered` `the person charged has been charged again with the offence or tried for it, so section 16 does not apply (s 16(1)(f)); the route depends on how that charge ends`
        IF `I am applying as` EQUALS `the person injured by the offence (the victim)`
            THEN IF `the victim suffered injury`
                 THEN `answered` (`yes: I may apply for compensation for my injury and any loss also suffered` `the section`)
                 ELSE `not covered` `sections 12 to 17 give a right to apply to a person who suffers injury (bodily harm, mental and nervous shock, or pregnancy); this Act does not speak to a person who suffered none`
        IF `a close relative suffered loss from the death`
            THEN `answered` (`yes: as the personal representative I may apply for compensation for the loss the close relatives suffered as a result of the death` `the section`)
        OTHERWISE `answered` `no: the personal representative may apply only if a close relative of the deceased suffered loss as a result of the death`
    WHERE
        `the section` MEANS `the section for` `what happened in the criminal case` `the date of the offence`

§§ `s 11 -- how do I apply`

-- s 11(1): "A compensation application must be -- (a) made in writing on a form approved by the Chief Assessor; and (b)
-- given to the Chief Assessor." s 11(2): "(a) under 18 years of age -- the application may be made on his or her behalf by
-- a parent, or a person acting in place of a parent, of the person; (b) a represented person within the meaning of the
-- Guardianship and Administration Act 1990 -- the application may be made on his or her behalf by the person's guardian or
-- administrator appointed under that Act."
DECLARE `How do I apply` IS ONE OF
    `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor (s 11(1))`
    `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am under 18, a parent or a person acting in place of a parent may make it for me (s 11(1), (2)(a))`
    `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am a represented person, my guardian or administrator may make it for me (s 11(1), (2)(b))`
    `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am under 18 and a represented person, a parent or a person acting in place of a parent, or my guardian or administrator, may make it for me (s 11(1), (2))`

@export How do I make a claim for criminal injuries compensation in Western Australia, and who may make it for me?
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc My age in whole years
      `I am a represented person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a guardian or administrator has been appointed for me under the Guardianship and Administration Act 1990
GIVETH A `How do I apply`
DECIDE `How do I apply question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `my age` LESS THAN 18 AND `I am a represented person`
            THEN `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am under 18 and a represented person, a parent or a person acting in place of a parent, or my guardian or administrator, may make it for me (s 11(1), (2))`
        IF `my age` LESS THAN 18
            THEN `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am under 18, a parent or a person acting in place of a parent may make it for me (s 11(1), (2)(a))`
        IF `I am a represented person`
            THEN `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor; because I am a represented person, my guardian or administrator may make it for me (s 11(1), (2)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `in writing, on the form approved by the Chief Assessor, given to the Chief Assessor (s 11(1))`

§§ `s 9 -- by when must I apply`

DECLARE `Compensation party` IS ONE OF
    `I, the applicant`
    `an assessor`
    `the District Court`

DECLARE `Compensation action` IS ONE OF
    `make the compensation application`
    `allow the application to be made after the 3 years`
    `commence an appeal to the District Court`
    `allow the appeal to be commenced after the 21 days`

-- s 9(1): "A compensation application must be made within 3 years after the date on which -- (a) the offence to which it
-- relates was committed; or (b) if it relates to more than one offence, the last of them was committed." s 9(2): "Despite
-- subsection (1), an assessor may allow a compensation application to be made after the 3 years if he or she thinks it is
-- just to do so and may do so on any conditions that he or she thinks it is just to impose."
-- DO, not MUST: missing the 3 years is not a wrong; it hands the question to an assessor. WITHIN counts days from the
-- offence (day 0).
GIVEN `days in the 3 years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Compensation party` `Compensation action`
DECIDE `s 9 -- apply within 3 years, or an assessor may allow it later` `days in the 3 years` IS
    PARTY `I, the applicant`
    DO    `make the compensation application`
    WITHIN `days in the 3 years`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST
        PARTY `an assessor`
        MAY   `allow the application to be made after the 3 years`

GIVEN `the date of the offence` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the 3 years after` `the date of the offence` MEANS `the date` 3 `years after` `the date of the offence`

GIVEN `the date of the offence` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Compensation party` `Compensation action`
`the s 9 rule for an offence on` `the date of the offence` MEANS
    `s 9 -- apply within 3 years, or an assessor may allow it later` (`days from` `the date of the offence` `to` (`the last day of the 3 years after` `the date of the offence`))

DECLARE `By when must I apply` IS ONE OF
    `in time: the application must be made by the last day of the 3 years (s 9(1))` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `late: the 3 years have ended; an assessor may allow the application if he or she thinks it just, on any conditions he or she thinks just to impose (s 9(2))` HAS `the 3 years ended on` IS A DATE
    `no: this Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8)`

@export By when must I apply for criminal injuries compensation in Western Australia, and what if I am late?
GIVEN `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was committed; if the claim relates to more than one offence, the date the last of them was committed (ISO 8601)
      `the date I apply` IS A DATE @desc The date the application is (or would be) given to the Chief Assessor, usually today (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `By when must I apply`
DECIDE `By when must I apply question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls before` `22 January 1971`
            THEN `no: this Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8)`
        IF `the date I apply` `falls on or before` (`the last day of the 3 years after` `the date of the offence`)
            THEN `in time: the application must be made by the last day of the 3 years (s 9(1))` (`the last day of the 3 years after` `the date of the offence`)
        OTHERWISE `late: the 3 years have ended; an assessor may allow the application if he or she thinks it just, on any conditions he or she thinks just to impose (s 9(2))` (`the last day of the 3 years after` `the date of the offence`)

§§ `s 10 -- does the entitlement end on death (arises only if the person entitled has died)`

-- s 10(1): "Any entitlement of a victim to compensation under this Act ceases on the death of the victim." s 10(2): "Any
-- entitlement of a close relative of a deceased victim to compensation under this Act ceases on the death of the close
-- relative." s 10(3): this applies even if the application was made before the death, or an award was made after and in
-- ignorance of the death.
DECLARE `Whose entitlement` IS ONE OF
    `the injured victim's own entitlement`
    `a close relative's entitlement for loss from the victim's death`

DECLARE `Does the entitlement survive` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the person entitled is alive, so section 10 does not end the entitlement`
    `no: the injured victim's entitlement ceased on the victim's death, even if an application was already made or an award made in ignorance of the death (s 10(1), (3))`
    `no: the close relative's entitlement ceased on the close relative's death, even if an application was already made or an award made in ignorance of the death (s 10(2), (3))`

@export Someone entitled to criminal injuries compensation in Western Australia has died. Does the entitlement pass on, or end?
GIVEN `whose entitlement` IS A `Whose entitlement` @desc Whose entitlement I am asking about: the injured victim's, or a close relative's for loss from a victim's death
      `that person has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person entitled (the victim or the close relative) has died, of any cause
GIVETH A `Does the entitlement survive`
DECIDE `Does the entitlement survive question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `that person has died`)
            THEN `yes: the person entitled is alive, so section 10 does not end the entitlement`
        IF `whose entitlement` EQUALS `the injured victim's own entitlement`
            THEN `no: the injured victim's entitlement ceased on the victim's death, even if an application was already made or an award made in ignorance of the death (s 10(1), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `no: the close relative's entitlement ceased on the close relative's death, even if an application was already made or an award made in ignorance of the death (s 10(2), (3))`

§§ `ss 6, 30 -- what can I be compensated for`

-- s 30(1): compensation "for the injury and for any loss also suffered"; s 30(3): for a death, "for the loss suffered by
-- the one or more close relatives of the deceased". s 6(2): "In the case of a victim who is injured, loss means -- (a)
-- expenses actually and reasonably incurred by or on behalf of the victim -- (i) that arise directly from; or (ii) that
-- arise in obtaining any report from a health professional or a counsellor in relation to, the injury suffered by the
-- victim; or (b) expenses that are likely to be reasonably incurred by or on behalf of the victim for treatment that the
-- victim is likely to need as a direct consequence of the injury suffered by the victim; or (c) loss of earnings suffered by
-- the victim as a direct consequence of the injury suffered by the victim; or (d) any loss arising from any damage caused
-- as a direct consequence of the commission of the offence to any personal item that was being worn by the victim when he
-- or she suffered the injury." s 6(1): "personal item means -- (a) an item of clothing or footwear; or (b) spectacles, or
-- contact lenses, used to correct eyesight; or (c) a hearing aid; or (d) artificial teeth; or (e) an artificial limb; or
-- (f) a surgical appliance or implant used to correct or relieve a physical disability or medical condition; or (g) any
-- other item prescribed by the regulations." s 6(3): "In the case of a victim who dies, loss means any loss suffered by a
-- close relative of the victim for which damages could be awarded to the relative under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 if the
-- death of the victim were caused by the wrongful act, neglect or default of another."
DECLARE `What I want compensation for` IS ONE OF
    `the injury itself (for example pain and suffering, or loss of enjoyment of life)`
    `expenses paid because of the injury (for example medical bills, travel to treatment)`
    `the cost of a report about the injury from a health professional or a counsellor`
    `treatment I am likely to need in future because of the injury`
    `earnings I lost because of the injury`
    `damage to something I was wearing when I was injured`
    `loss of or damage to other property (for example a phone in my bag, cash taken, my car)`
    `a close relative's loss from the victim's death`

DECLARE `The item I was wearing` IS ONE OF
    `clothing or footwear`
    `spectacles or contact lenses`
    `a hearing aid`
    `artificial teeth`
    `an artificial limb`
    `a surgical appliance or implant`
    `some other item (for example a watch, jewellery or a phone)`

DECLARE `Can I be compensated for it` IS ONE OF
    `yes: compensation may be awarded for the injury itself, as much as the assessor is satisfied is just (s 30(1))`
    `yes, if the expenses were actually and reasonably incurred and arise directly from the injury (s 6(2)(a)(i))`
    `yes, if the expense was actually and reasonably incurred (s 6(2)(a)(ii))`
    `yes, if the expenses are likely to be reasonably incurred for treatment I am likely to need as a direct consequence of the injury (s 6(2)(b)); the amount is paid only on a request made in time under section 48`
    `yes, if the earnings were lost as a direct consequence of the injury (s 6(2)(c))`
    `yes, if the damage was a direct consequence of the offence: the item is a personal item and I was wearing it when injured (s 6(1), (2)(d))`
    `no: damage to a personal item counts only if I was wearing it when I was injured (s 6(2)(d))`
    `no: property other than a personal item I was wearing is not "loss" under section 6(2)`
    `yes, for a close relative's loss of a kind for which damages could be awarded under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (s 6(3), s 30(3))`
    `no: the injured victim's claim is for the injury and the victim's own loss; a close relative's loss from a death is claimed by the deceased's personal representative (s 30(1), (3))`
    `no: on a death, the personal representative's claim is for the close relatives' loss from the death (s 6(3), s 30(3))`

@export I was hurt by a crime in Western Australia. What can criminal injuries compensation cover: pain, medical bills, lost wages, damaged belongings?
GIVEN `I am applying as` IS AN `I am applying as` @desc Whether I apply as the person injured, or as the personal representative of someone who died from the offence
      `what I want compensation for` IS A `What I want compensation for` @desc The kind of harm or cost I want compensation for
      `the item` IS A `The item I was wearing` @desc Only for damaged belongings: what the item is
      `I was wearing it when injured` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for damaged belongings: whether I was wearing the item when I was injured
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I be compensated for it`
DECIDE `Can I be compensated for it question` IS
    IF `I am applying as` EQUALS `the personal representative of a person who died as a consequence of the offence`
        THEN IF `what I want compensation for` EQUALS `a close relative's loss from the victim's death`
             THEN `answered` `yes, for a close relative's loss of a kind for which damages could be awarded under the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (s 6(3), s 30(3))`
             ELSE `answered` `no: on a death, the personal representative's claim is for the close relatives' loss from the death (s 6(3), s 30(3))`
        ELSE CONSIDER `what I want compensation for`
             WHEN `the injury itself (for example pain and suffering, or loss of enjoyment of life)` THEN `answered` `yes: compensation may be awarded for the injury itself, as much as the assessor is satisfied is just (s 30(1))`
             WHEN `expenses paid because of the injury (for example medical bills, travel to treatment)` THEN `answered` `yes, if the expenses were actually and reasonably incurred and arise directly from the injury (s 6(2)(a)(i))`
             WHEN `the cost of a report about the injury from a health professional or a counsellor` THEN `answered` `yes, if the expense was actually and reasonably incurred (s 6(2)(a)(ii))`
             WHEN `treatment I am likely to need in future because of the injury` THEN `answered` `yes, if the expenses are likely to be reasonably incurred for treatment I am likely to need as a direct consequence of the injury (s 6(2)(b)); the amount is paid only on a request made in time under section 48`
             WHEN `earnings I lost because of the injury` THEN `answered` `yes, if the earnings were lost as a direct consequence of the injury (s 6(2)(c))`
             WHEN `damage to something I was wearing when I was injured` THEN `the personal item answer`
             WHEN `loss of or damage to other property (for example a phone in my bag, cash taken, my car)` THEN `answered` `no: property other than a personal item I was wearing is not "loss" under section 6(2)`
             WHEN `a close relative's loss from the victim's death` THEN `answered` `no: the injured victim's claim is for the injury and the victim's own loss; a close relative's loss from a death is claimed by the deceased's personal representative (s 30(1), (3))`
    WHERE
        `the personal item answer` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF (NOT `I was wearing it when injured`)
                    THEN `answered` `no: damage to a personal item counts only if I was wearing it when I was injured (s 6(2)(d))`
                IF `the item` EQUALS `some other item (for example a watch, jewellery or a phone)`
                    THEN `not covered` `section 6(1)(g) lets regulations prescribe further personal items, and the regulations are not deposited`
                OTHERWISE `answered` `yes, if the damage was a direct consequence of the offence: the item is a personal item and I was wearing it when injured (s 6(1), (2)(d))`

§§ `ss 31-34 -- what is the most I can be awarded`

-- s 31(1) Table: the maximum for a single offence by the period in which it was committed (all dates inclusive).
--   1. 22 January 1971 to 17 October 1976: for an indictable offence $2 000; for a simple offence $300
--   2. 18 October 1976 to 31 December 1982: $7 500
--   3. 1 January 1983 to 31 December 1985: $15 000
--   4. 1 January 1986 to 30 June 1991: $20 000
--   5. 1 July 1991 to the day before this Act came into operation (31 December 2003): $50 000
--   6. On or after the day this Act came into operation (1 January 2004): $75 000
-- s 31(2): the same Table sets the maximum, in aggregate, on an application by the personal representative of a victim
-- who dies. s 31(3) lets regulations add items; none is deposited or noted in the compilation.
GIVEN `the date of the offence` IS A DATE, `the offence was indictable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 31 Table maximum for an offence on` `the date of the offence` `the offence was indictable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls on or before` (`the day` 17 10 1976)
            THEN IF `the offence was indictable` THEN 2000 ELSE 300
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls on or before` (`the day` 31 12 1982)
            THEN 7500
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls on or before` (`the day` 31 12 1985)
            THEN 15000
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls on or before` (`the day` 30 6 1991)
            THEN 20000
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls before` `the day this Act came into operation`
            THEN 50000
        OTHERWISE 75000

-- s 32: the single-offence maximum applies "even if the single offence is committed by 2 or more persons acting in
-- concert". s 33(1): offences are related "if an assessor is satisfied -- (a) that they were committed at approximately the
-- same time ...; or (b) that they are related for any other reason". s 33(2): for related offences the amounts "must not
-- in aggregate exceed the maximum amount that may be awarded for the last one of the offences to be committed". s 33(3):
-- "twice the maximum" for a close relative of 2 or more victims who die from related offences, as a result of 2 or more of
-- those deaths. s 34: "twice the maximum ... for the last one of the offences" for 2 or more unrelated offences committed
-- by one person, on an application made on or after 23 September 2003.
DECLARE `The offences` IS ONE OF
    `a single offence (even if committed by two or more people acting in concert)`
    `two or more related offences (as an assessor is satisfied: committed at about the same time, or related for another reason)`
    `two or more related offences from which two or more of my close relatives died, and I claim as a result of two or more of those deaths`
    `two or more offences committed by one person that are not related`

@export What is the most I can be awarded in criminal injuries compensation in Western Australia?
GIVEN `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was committed; if more than one, the date the last of them was committed (ISO 8601)
      `the offence was indictable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for an offence between 22 January 1971 and 17 October 1976: whether it was an indictable offence (a crime or misdemeanour) rather than a simple offence
      `the offences` IS A `The offences` @desc Whether the claim is for one offence or several, and how they are connected
      `the date I applied` IS A DATE @desc The date the compensation application was made (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be awarded question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date of the offence` `falls before` `22 January 1971`
            THEN `not covered` `the Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8), so it sets no maximum`
        IF    `the offences` EQUALS `a single offence (even if committed by two or more people acting in concert)`
           OR `the offences` EQUALS `two or more related offences (as an assessor is satisfied: committed at about the same time, or related for another reason)`
            THEN `answered` `the Table maximum`
        IF `the offences` EQUALS `two or more related offences from which two or more of my close relatives died, and I claim as a result of two or more of those deaths`
            THEN `answered` (2 TIMES `the Table maximum`)
        IF `the date I applied` `falls on or after` `23 September 2003`
            THEN `answered` (2 TIMES `the Table maximum`)
        OTHERWISE `not covered` `section 34 applies to an application made on or after 23 September 2003; for an earlier application this Act does not say what the maximum is for unrelated offences by one offender`
    WHERE
        `the Table maximum` MEANS `the s 31 Table maximum for an offence on` `the date of the offence` `the offence was indictable`

§§ `s 35 -- can I be compensated for mental and nervous shock`

-- s 35(1): applies to an application made on or after 23 September 2003. s 35(2): "An assessor must not make a
-- compensation award for mental and nervous shock suffered by a victim ... unless the assessor is satisfied -- (a) that the
-- victim also suffered bodily harm or became pregnant as a consequence of the commission of the offence; or (b) that the
-- victim was the person against whom, or against whose property, the offence was committed; or (c) that a person other
-- than the victim died or suffered injury as a consequence of the offence and the victim was personally present when or
-- immediately after the offence was committed; or (d) that immediately before the offence was committed the victim was the
-- parent or stepparent of a person who died as a consequence of the commission of the offence; or (e) that immediately
-- before the offence was committed the victim -- (i) was a close relative of a person who suffered injury or died as a
-- consequence of the commission of the offence; and (ii) was living with that person." s 35(3): "Despite subsection (2),
-- if an assessor is satisfied -- (a) that a person died or was injured as a consequence of the commission of an offence;
-- and (b) that the death occurred or the injury was suffered when the person was committing a separate offence, the
-- assessor must not make a compensation award in favour of a close relative of the person for mental and nervous shock
-- suffered by the close relative as a result of the death or injury."
DECLARE `Can I be compensated for shock` IS ONE OF
    `section 35 does not stand in the way: it applies to applications made on or after 23 September 2003 (s 35(1))`
    `no: the person who died or was injured was committing a separate offence, and no award can be made to a close relative for mental and nervous shock from that death or injury (s 35(3))`
    `section 35 allows it: I also suffered bodily harm or became pregnant (s 35(2)(a))`
    `section 35 allows it: the offence was committed against me or my property (s 35(2)(b))`
    `section 35 allows it: someone else died or was injured and I was personally present when or immediately after the offence was committed (s 35(2)(c))`
    `section 35 allows it: I was the parent or stepparent of a person who died from the offence (s 35(2)(d))`
    `section 35 allows it: I was a close relative of a person injured or killed by the offence and living with them (s 35(2)(e))`
    `no: an award for mental and nervous shock needs one of the links in section 35(2), and none is met`

@export I suffered mental and nervous shock (psychological injury) from a crime in Western Australia. Can I be compensated for it?
GIVEN `the date I applied` IS A DATE @desc The date the compensation application was made (ISO 8601)
      `I also suffered bodily harm or became pregnant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I also suffered bodily harm, or became pregnant, as a consequence of the offence
      `the offence was against me or my property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I was the person against whom, or against whose property, the offence was committed
      `someone else died or was injured and I was there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone else died or was injured by the offence and I was personally present when, or immediately after, it was committed
      `I was the parent or stepparent of someone it killed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before the offence, I was the parent or stepparent of a person who died from it
      `I was a close relative living with someone it injured or killed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, immediately before the offence, I was a close relative of a person injured or killed by it and was living with them
      `the person hurt was committing a separate offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether my shock comes from the death or injury of a close relative who was, as an assessor is satisfied, committing a separate offence when it happened
GIVETH A `Can I be compensated for shock`
DECIDE `Can I be compensated for shock question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date I applied` `falls before` `23 September 2003`
            THEN `section 35 does not stand in the way: it applies to applications made on or after 23 September 2003 (s 35(1))`
        IF `the person hurt was committing a separate offence`
            THEN `no: the person who died or was injured was committing a separate offence, and no award can be made to a close relative for mental and nervous shock from that death or injury (s 35(3))`
        IF `I also suffered bodily harm or became pregnant`
            THEN `section 35 allows it: I also suffered bodily harm or became pregnant (s 35(2)(a))`
        IF `the offence was against me or my property`
            THEN `section 35 allows it: the offence was committed against me or my property (s 35(2)(b))`
        IF `someone else died or was injured and I was there`
            THEN `section 35 allows it: someone else died or was injured and I was personally present when or immediately after the offence was committed (s 35(2)(c))`
        IF `I was the parent or stepparent of someone it killed`
            THEN `section 35 allows it: I was the parent or stepparent of a person who died from the offence (s 35(2)(d))`
        IF `I was a close relative living with someone it injured or killed`
            THEN `section 35 allows it: I was a close relative of a person injured or killed by the offence and living with them (s 35(2)(e))`
        OTHERWISE `no: an award for mental and nervous shock needs one of the links in section 35(2), and none is met`

§§ `ss 36-41 -- is anything stopping an award to me`

-- s 36: no award "if the assessor is of the opinion -- (a) that there is a relationship or connection between the person
-- who committed the offence and the victim or close relative; and (b) that by reason of the relationship or connection any
-- money paid under the award is likely to benefit or advantage the person who committed the offence."
-- s 37 (offences on or after 1 July 1993): no award "if satisfied that the injury or death was caused directly by, or by
-- the driving or other use of, a motor vehicle unless -- (a) the motor vehicle was used for the purpose of committing the
-- offence; and (b) the offence is a crime."
-- s 38: no award "if the assessor is of the opinion that the victim or close relative did not do any act or thing which
-- he or she ought reasonably to have done to assist in the identification, apprehension or prosecution of the person who
-- committed the offence."
-- s 39(1): no award to a person injured "when the person was committing a separate offence"; s 39(2): no award to a close
-- relative for loss from a death that "occurred when the person was committing a separate offence".
-- s 40: no award if an award has been made previously, or an application refused previously, for the injury (or, for a
-- close relative, the loss from the death), under this Act or the 1970, 1982 or 1985 Acts.
-- s 41: the assessor "must have regard to any behaviour, condition, attitude, or disposition of the victim that contributed
-- ... to the victim's injury or death" and may, if just, refuse an award or reduce it.
DECLARE `Bar` IS ONE OF
    `s 36: because of a relationship or connection between the offender and the victim or close relative, money paid under the award is likely to benefit or advantage the offender`
    `s 37: the injury or death was caused directly by, or by the driving or other use of, a motor vehicle, and the vehicle was not used to commit an offence that is a crime`
    `s 38: the victim or close relative did not do something they ought reasonably to have done to help identify, apprehend or prosecute the offender`
    `s 39(1): I was injured when I was committing a separate offence`
    `s 39(2): the person who died was committing a separate offence when they died`
    `s 40: an award has already been made, or an application refused, for this injury or loss`
    `s 41: the victim's behaviour, condition, attitude or disposition contributed to the injury or death, and an assessor thinks it just to refuse an award because of it`

@export I am claiming criminal injuries compensation in Western Australia. Is there anything in the Act that stops an award being made to me?
GIVEN `I am applying as` IS AN `I am applying as` @desc Whether I apply as the person injured, or as the personal representative of someone who died from the offence
      `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was committed (ISO 8601)
      `the assessor thinks the award would benefit the offender` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an assessor is of the opinion that, because of a relationship or connection between the offender and the victim or close relative, money paid under the award is likely to benefit or advantage the offender
      `a motor vehicle caused the injury or death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, as an assessor is satisfied, the injury or death was caused directly by, or by the driving or other use of, a motor vehicle
      `the vehicle was used to commit the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the motor vehicle was used for the purpose of committing the offence (for example deliberately driven at the victim)
      `the offence is a crime` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offence is a crime (the most serious class of offence in Western Australia, as opposed to a simple offence)
      `the assessor thinks there was no reasonable help to investigators` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an assessor is of the opinion that the victim or close relative failed to do something they ought reasonably to have done to help identify, apprehend or prosecute the offender
      `the victim was committing a separate offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, as an assessor is satisfied, the injury was suffered, or the death occurred, while the victim was committing a separate offence
      `an award was made or refused before for this` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an award has already been made, or an application refused, for this injury or loss, under this Act or an earlier criminal injuries compensation Act
      `the assessor refuses because of the victim's contribution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an assessor, having regard to the victim's behaviour, condition, attitude or disposition that contributed to the injury or death, thinks it just to refuse an award because of it
GIVETH A LIST OF `Bar`
DECIDE `What stops an award question` IS
    catMaybes (LIST
        (IF `the assessor thinks the award would benefit the offender` THEN JUST `s 36: because of a relationship or connection between the offender and the victim or close relative, money paid under the award is likely to benefit or advantage the offender` ELSE NOTHING),
        (IF `the motor vehicle bar applies` THEN JUST `s 37: the injury or death was caused directly by, or by the driving or other use of, a motor vehicle, and the vehicle was not used to commit an offence that is a crime` ELSE NOTHING),
        (IF `the assessor thinks there was no reasonable help to investigators` THEN JUST `s 38: the victim or close relative did not do something they ought reasonably to have done to help identify, apprehend or prosecute the offender` ELSE NOTHING),
        (IF `the victim was committing a separate offence` AND `I am the injured victim` THEN JUST `s 39(1): I was injured when I was committing a separate offence` ELSE NOTHING),
        (IF `the victim was committing a separate offence` AND (NOT `I am the injured victim`) THEN JUST `s 39(2): the person who died was committing a separate offence when they died` ELSE NOTHING),
        (IF `an award was made or refused before for this` THEN JUST `s 40: an award has already been made, or an application refused, for this injury or loss` ELSE NOTHING),
        (IF `the assessor refuses because of the victim's contribution` THEN JUST `s 41: the victim's behaviour, condition, attitude or disposition contributed to the injury or death, and an assessor thinks it just to refuse an award because of it` ELSE NOTHING))
    WHERE
        `I am the injured victim` MEANS `I am applying as` EQUALS `the person injured by the offence (the victim)`
        `the motor vehicle bar applies` MEANS
                `the date of the offence` `falls on or after` `1 July 1993`
            AND `a motor vehicle caused the injury or death`
            AND (NOT (`the vehicle was used to commit the offence` AND `the offence is a crime`))

§§ `s 23 -- interim payments`

-- s 23(1): an assessor satisfied that expenses have been or are likely to be incurred, and that an interim payment is
-- warranted, may make an interim payment. s 23(2): "the total of the payments, not including any payment for the funeral of
-- a victim who has died, must not exceed 3% of the maximum amount of compensation that could be awarded if the application
-- were for compensation in relation to a single offence." s 23(3): interim payments are deducted from any award. s 23(4):
-- if an award is then refused, the interim payments become a debt due to the State.
@export What is the most I can be paid in interim payments before my criminal injuries compensation claim in Western Australia is decided?
GIVEN `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was committed; if more than one, the date the last of them was committed (ISO 8601)
      `the offence was indictable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for an offence between 22 January 1971 and 17 October 1976: whether it was an indictable offence (a crime or misdemeanour) rather than a simple offence
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
DECIDE `Most in interim payments question` IS
    IF `the date of the offence` `falls before` `22 January 1971`
        THEN `not covered` `the Act does not apply to an offence committed before 22 January 1971 (s 8), so it sets no maximum`
        ELSE `answered` ((`the s 31 Table maximum for an offence on` `the date of the offence` `the offence was indictable`) TIMES 0.03)

§§ `ss 20(2)(d), 23(3), 31-34, 42, 43 -- how much will I be paid`

-- s 30(1), (3): the assessor may award "such compensation that the assessor is satisfied is just" (asked as an input,
-- after the assessor's regard to s 29, s 20(3) and s 41). ss 31-34: not above the maximum. Then the award is reduced by:
-- s 20(2)(d): "any compensation award must be reduced by the amount so paid" (the examination the assessor directed);
-- s 23(3): "The total of any interim payments must be deducted from any compensation award";
-- s 42(2): "any amount that the victim or close relative would, but for this Act, also be entitled to receive under a
-- contract of insurance with a registered organisation [a private health insurer] in respect of any of that loss";
-- s 42(3): "any amount that the victim or close relative has received, whether under a contract of insurance or otherwise,
-- for the injury or loss" (must deduct); s 42(4): an amount the victim or close relative "will receive" (may deduct);
-- s 42(5): "in the case of an application by a personal representative of a deceased victim, the amounts described in the
-- Fatal Accidents Act 1959 section 5(2)(b) and (c) must not be deducted" under (3) and (4).
-- s 43: the assessor "may reduce the amount payable ... by an amount not exceeding any amount that the victim or close
-- relative owes the State" under s 23(4), a compensation reimbursement order or s 68.
-- The maximum is applied to the award first and the deductions are taken from the award (fork register in NOTES.md).
GIVEN `the just amount` IS A NUMBER, `the maximum` IS A NUMBER, `the deductions` IS A NUMBER, `the set off` IS A NUMBER, `owed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount payable from` `the just amount` `the maximum` `the deductions` `the set off` `owed` MEANS
    `the award less deductions` MINUS (min (min `the set off` `owed`) `the award less deductions`)
    WHERE
        `the award less deductions` MEANS max 0 ((min `the just amount` `the maximum`) MINUS `the deductions`)

@export How much criminal injuries compensation will I actually be paid in Western Australia, after the maximum and the deductions?
GIVEN `I am applying as` IS AN `I am applying as` @desc Whether I apply as the person injured, or as the personal representative of someone who died from the offence
      `the date of the offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence was committed; if more than one, the date the last of them was committed (ISO 8601)
      `the offence was indictable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for an offence between 22 January 1971 and 17 October 1976: whether it was an indictable offence rather than a simple offence
      `the offences` IS A `The offences` @desc Whether the claim is for one offence or several, and how they are connected
      `the date I applied` IS A DATE @desc The date the compensation application was made (ISO 8601)
      `the amount the assessor is satisfied is just` IS A NUMBER @desc The compensation, in dollars, the assessor is satisfied is just for the injury and loss (or the close relatives' loss), before the maximum and the deductions
      `the cost of an examination the assessor directed` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars the Chief Assessor paid for a health professional's examination and report the assessor directed (0 if none)
      `interim payments I received` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of interim payments already made on this application, including any for a funeral (0 if none)
      `what my private health insurer must pay for the loss` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars I would be entitled to from a private health insurer (a registered organisation under the National Health Act 1953) for the loss, not yet received (0 if none)
      `what I have already received for the injury or loss` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars already received for the injury or loss from any other source, such as insurance, damages or other compensation (0 if none)
      `what the assessor deducts for what I will receive` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars the assessor, satisfied I will receive them for the injury or loss from another source, decides to deduct (0 if none)
      `the Fatal Accidents Act part of those` IS A NUMBER @desc For a personal representative only: dollars of the amounts received or to be received that are of the kind in the Fatal Accidents Act 1959 s 5(2)(b) or (c), such as life insurance or superannuation paid on the death (0 if none)
      `what I owe the State` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars I owe the State under s 23(4) (interim payments on a refused claim), a compensation reimbursement order, or s 68 (0 if none)
      `the amount the assessor sets off` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars the assessor decides to set off against what I owe the State (0 if none)
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
DECIDE `How much will I be paid question` IS
    CONSIDER `What is the most I can be awarded question` `the date of the offence` `the offence was indictable` `the offences` `the date I applied`
        WHEN `answered` `the maximum` THEN `answered` (`the amount payable from` `the amount the assessor is satisfied is just` `the maximum` `the deductions` `the amount the assessor sets off` `what I owe the State`)
        WHEN `not covered` `the reason` THEN `not covered` `the reason`
    WHERE
        `the s 42(3) and (4) amounts` MEANS
            IF `I am applying as` EQUALS `the personal representative of a person who died as a consequence of the offence`
                THEN max 0 ((`what I have already received for the injury or loss` PLUS `what the assessor deducts for what I will receive`) MINUS `the Fatal Accidents Act part of those`)
                ELSE `what I have already received for the injury or loss` PLUS `what the assessor deducts for what I will receive`
        `the deductions` MEANS
            `the cost of an examination the assessor directed` PLUS `interim payments I received` PLUS `what my private health insurer must pay for the loss` PLUS `the s 42(3) and (4) amounts`

§§ `s 47 -- when will I be paid (arises only once an award is made)`

-- s 47: "Payment under a compensation award is not to be made until -- (a) the period referred to in section 55(3) has
-- elapsed; and (b) any appeal commenced under Part 7 is determined, except with the written authority of an assessor."
DECLARE `When can I be paid` IS ONE OF
    `now: an assessor has given written authority for payment (s 47)`
    `now: the 21 days for an appeal have elapsed and no appeal is awaiting decision (s 47)`
    `not yet: payment waits until the 21 days for an appeal have elapsed (s 47(a), s 55(3))` HAS `the 21 days end on` IS A DATE
    `not yet: payment waits until the appeal is determined (s 47(b))`

GIVEN `the date of the decision` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to appeal against a decision on` `the date of the decision` MEANS `the date` 21 `days after` `the date of the decision`

@export I have been awarded criminal injuries compensation in Western Australia. When can it be paid to me?
GIVEN `the date of the award` IS A DATE @desc The date of the assessor's decision making the award (ISO 8601)
      `the date I am asking about` IS A DATE @desc The date to answer for, usually today (ISO 8601)
      `an appeal has been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether anyone has commenced an appeal to the District Court against the decision
      `the appeal has been determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If an appeal was commenced, whether the District Court has decided it
      `an assessor has authorised payment in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an assessor has given written authority for the payment to be made sooner
GIVETH A `When can I be paid`
DECIDE `When can I be paid question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `an assessor has authorised payment in writing`
            THEN `now: an assessor has given written authority for payment (s 47)`
        IF `an appeal has been commenced` AND (NOT `the appeal has been determined`)
            THEN `not yet: payment waits until the appeal is determined (s 47(b))`
        IF `the date I am asking about` `falls on or before` (`the last day to appeal against a decision on` `the date of the award`)
            THEN `not yet: payment waits until the 21 days for an appeal have elapsed (s 47(a), s 55(3))` (`the last day to appeal against a decision on` `the date of the award`)
        OTHERWISE `now: the 21 days for an appeal have elapsed and no appeal is awaiting decision (s 47)`

§§ `s 55 -- can I appeal, and by when (arises only once the assessor decides)`

-- s 55(1): "An interested person may appeal to the District Court against an assessor's decision -- (a) to make or to
-- refuse to make a compensation award; (b) as to the amount of a compensation award." (The applicant is an interested
-- person: s 3.) s 55(3): "The appeal must be commenced within 21 days after the date of the decision." s 55(4): "If it is
-- just to do so, the District Court may allow an appeal to be commenced after the 21 days, and may do so even if the period
-- has expired." s 57: the District Court's decision is final.
-- DO, not MUST: not appealing in time is not a wrong; it hands the question to the District Court. WITHIN counts days
-- from the decision (day 0).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Compensation party` `Compensation action`
DECIDE `s 55 -- appeal within 21 days, or the District Court may allow it later` IS
    PARTY `I, the applicant`
    DO    `commence an appeal to the District Court`
    WITHIN 21
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST
        PARTY `the District Court`
        MAY   `allow the appeal to be commenced after the 21 days`

DECLARE `What I want to appeal against` IS ONE OF
    `the decision to make, or to refuse to make, an award`
    `the amount of the award`
    `some other decision (for example to defer, stay or require me to take other proceedings)`

DECLARE `Can I appeal` IS ONE OF
    `yes, in time: I may appeal to the District Court; the appeal must be commenced by the last day of the 21 days (s 55(1), (3)); its decision is final (s 57)` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `late: the 21 days have ended; the District Court may allow the appeal to be commenced if it is just to do so (s 55(4)); its decision is final (s 57)` HAS `the 21 days ended on` IS A DATE

@export I disagree with the assessor's decision on my criminal injuries compensation claim in Western Australia. Can I appeal, and by when?
GIVEN `what I want to appeal against` IS A `What I want to appeal against` @desc Which decision of the assessor I want to challenge
      `the date of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The date of the assessor's decision (ISO 8601)
      `the date I would appeal` IS A DATE @desc The date the appeal is (or would be) commenced in the District Court, usually today (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I appeal`
DECIDE `Can I appeal question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `what I want to appeal against` EQUALS `some other decision (for example to defer, stay or require me to take other proceedings)`
            THEN `not covered` `section 55(1) gives the applicant an appeal against a decision to make or refuse an award, and as to its amount; this Act does not speak to an appeal against other decisions`
        IF `the date I would appeal` `falls on or before` (`the last day to appeal against a decision on` `the date of the decision`)
            THEN `answered` (`yes, in time: I may appeal to the District Court; the appeal must be commenced by the last day of the 21 days (s 55(1), (3)); its decision is final (s 57)` (`the last day to appeal against a decision on` `the date of the decision`))
        OTHERWISE `answered` (`late: the 21 days have ended; the District Court may allow the appeal to be commenced if it is just to do so (s 55(4)); its decision is final (s 57)` (`the last day to appeal against a decision on` `the date of the decision`))

§§ `ss 26, 27 -- will I be given the decision and reasons (arises only once the assessor decides)`

-- s 26: an award, or a decision to refuse one, must be made in writing and given to the applicant. s 27(1): for an award,
-- written reasons go to an interested person "who, in writing, asks"; s 27(2): for a refusal, written reasons must be given
-- to the applicant (and to any other interested person who asks in writing).
DECLARE `The assessor's decision` IS ONE OF
    `an award was made`
    `the award was refused`

DECLARE `Will I be given reasons` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I must be given the refusal in writing, with written reasons, whether or not I ask (ss 26, 27(2)(a))`
    `yes: I must be given the award in writing, and, because I asked in writing, written reasons for it (ss 26, 27(1))`
    `I must be given the award in writing (s 26); written reasons must be given if I ask for them in writing (s 27(1))`

@export Will I be given the assessor's decision on my criminal injuries compensation claim in Western Australia, and the reasons for it?
GIVEN `the assessor's decision` IS A `The assessor's decision` @desc Whether the assessor made an award or refused one
      `I asked in writing for reasons` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether I have asked the assessor in writing for the reasons
GIVETH A `Will I be given reasons`
DECIDE `Will I be given reasons question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the assessor's decision` EQUALS `the award was refused`
            THEN `yes: I must be given the refusal in writing, with written reasons, whether or not I ask (ss 26, 27(2)(a))`
        IF `I asked in writing for reasons`
            THEN `yes: I must be given the award in writing, and, because I asked in writing, written reasons for it (ss 26, 27(1))`
        OTHERWISE `I must be given the award in writing (s 26); written reasons must be given if I ask for them in writing (s 27(1))`

§§ `s 48 -- future treatment expenses (arises only if my award includes them)`

-- s 48(1): an amount awarded for expenses "likely to be reasonably incurred ... for treatment" (s 6(2)(b)) "is not to be
-- paid unless" evidence is given to the Chief Assessor, an assessor is satisfied the expenses were reasonably incurred for
-- treatment the victim required as a direct consequence of the injury, and a request for payment is given in time.
-- s 48(2): the request must be given "(a) if the victim was under 18 years of age on the date of the award, before -- (i)
-- the victim reaches 28 years of age; or (ii) the expiry of 10 years after the date on which the Acts Amendment (Justice)
-- Act 2008 section 31 commences, whichever occurs last; or (b) otherwise -- (i) if the award was made before that section
-- commences, within 10 years after the day on which it commences; or (ii) otherwise, within 10 years after the date of the
-- award." Section 31 commenced on 31 July 2008, so the 10 years of (a)(ii) and (b)(i) end on 31 July 2018. "Before the
-- victim reaches 28" is read as by the day before the 28th birthday.
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE, `the date of the award` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to request future treatment money` `my date of birth` `the date of the award` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date of the award` `falls before` (`the date` 18 `years after` `my date of birth`)
            THEN `the later of` (`the eve of` (`the date` 28 `years after` `my date of birth`)) `and` `the end of the 10 years after 31 July 2008`
        IF `the date of the award` `falls before` `the day the Acts Amendment (Justice) Act 2008 section 31 commenced`
            THEN `the end of the 10 years after 31 July 2008`
        OTHERWISE `the date` 10 `years after` `the date of the award`
    WHERE
        `the end of the 10 years after 31 July 2008` MEANS `the date` 10 `years after` `the day the Acts Amendment (Justice) Act 2008 section 31 commenced`
        GIVEN d IS A DATE
        GIVETH A DATE
        `the eve of` d MEANS `the date` (0 MINUS 1) `days after` d

DECLARE `Can I still ask for future treatment money` IS ONE OF
    `yes: give the Chief Assessor the request, with evidence that the treatment expenses were reasonably incurred as a direct consequence of the injury, by the last day (s 48(1), (2))` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `no: the time to give the request ended, so the amount for future treatment is not to be paid (s 48(1)(c), (2))` HAS `the time ended on` IS A DATE

@export My criminal injuries compensation award in Western Australia includes money for future treatment. By when must I ask for it to be paid?
GIVEN `my date of birth` IS A DATE @desc The injured victim's date of birth (ISO 8601)
      `the date of the award` IS A DATE @desc The date the award was made (ISO 8601)
      `the date I give the request` IS A DATE @desc The date the request for payment is (or would be) given to the Chief Assessor, usually today (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `Can I still ask for future treatment money`
DECIDE `Can I still ask for future treatment money question` IS
    IF `the date I give the request` `falls on or before` `the last day`
        THEN `yes: give the Chief Assessor the request, with evidence that the treatment expenses were reasonably incurred as a direct consequence of the injury, by the last day (s 48(1), (2))` `the last day`
        ELSE `no: the time to give the request ended, so the amount for future treatment is not to be paid (s 48(1)(c), (2))` `the last day`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `the last day to request future treatment money` `my date of birth` `the date of the award`

§§ `ss 23(4), 68 -- what may I have to pay back to the State`

-- s 23(4): "If one or more interim payments are made to a person and a compensation award in favour of that person is
-- subsequently refused, the total of the interim payments becomes a debt due to the State by the person."
-- s 68(1): if an award is made, the victim or close relative "also receives or recovers in respect of that injury or loss
-- an amount under a contract of insurance or by way of damages or compensation, otherwise than under this Act", and that
-- amount was not deducted under s 42(3) or (4), "an amount equal to the lesser of -- (d) the amount awarded ...; or (e) the
-- amount referred to in paragraph (b), is a debt due to the State". s 68(1A): not if the other amount "was reduced to take
-- into account the compensation award".
@export I applied for criminal injuries compensation in Western Australia. How much may I have to pay back to the State?
GIVEN `interim payments made to me` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars of interim payments made to me on the application (0 if none)
      `my award was refused` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the assessor refused to make an award in my favour
      `the amount awarded to me` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars awarded to me (0 if refused)
      `what I received or recovered otherwise` IS A NUMBER @desc Dollars I have also received or recovered for the same injury or loss under insurance, as damages or as other compensation (0 if none)
      `that amount was deducted from my award` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that amount was deducted from my award under s 42(3) or (4)
      `that amount was reduced because of my award` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that other payment was itself reduced to take account of my award
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What must I pay back question` IS
    IF `my award was refused`
        THEN `interim payments made to me`
        ELSE IF `that amount was deducted from my award` OR `that amount was reduced because of my award`
             THEN 0
             ELSE min `the amount awarded to me` `what I received or recovered otherwise`

§§ `ss 19(5), 63(5), 64(5), 70, 71 -- offences an applicant can commit (arises only if accused of one)`

-- s 19(5): not complying with an assessor's notice to give information, appear or produce records, without a reasonable
-- excuse: $5 000. s 63(5): a witness not appearing, not taking an oath or affirmation, or not giving evidence or producing
-- a record, without a reasonable excuse: $5 000. s 64(5): contravening an order restricting publication: $5 000 for a
-- natural person ($25 000 for a body corporate, not this seat). s 70(2): giving information in an application, or in
-- answer to a s 19 request or notice, "knowing that it is false in a material particular": $5 000, and the court may also
-- order repayment of any compensation paid on the application (s 70(3)). s 71: a prosecution "must be commenced within 2
-- years after the date on which the offence is alleged to have been committed".
DECLARE `What I am accused of` IS ONE OF
    `not complying with an assessor's notice to give information, appear or produce a record (s 19(5))`
    `as a witness, not appearing, not taking an oath or affirmation, or not giving evidence or producing a record (s 63(5))`
    `publishing in breach of an assessor's order restricting publication (s 64(5))`
    `giving information I knew was false in a material particular (s 70(2))`

DECLARE `What I face` IS ONE OF
    `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (s 71)` HAS `the most I can be fined` IS A NUMBER, `the last day` IS A DATE
    `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount and an order to repay any compensation paid on the application; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (ss 70, 71)` HAS `the most I can be fined` IS A NUMBER, `the last day` IS A DATE
    `no prosecution: the 2 years for commencing one have ended (s 71)` HAS `the 2 years ended on` IS A DATE

@export I am accused of an offence under the Criminal Injuries Compensation Act 2003 (WA). What is the most I can be fined, and can I still be prosecuted?
GIVEN `what I am accused of` IS A `What I am accused of` @desc The offence I am accused of under this Act
      `the date of the alleged offence` IS A DATE @desc The date the offence is alleged to have been committed (ISO 8601)
      `the date the prosecution is commenced` IS A DATE @desc The date the prosecution was (or would be) commenced, usually today (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A `What I face`
DECIDE `What do I face question` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date the prosecution is commenced` `falls after` `the last day`
            THEN `no prosecution: the 2 years for commencing one have ended (s 71)` `the last day`
        IF `what I am accused of` EQUALS `giving information I knew was false in a material particular (s 70(2))`
            THEN `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount and an order to repay any compensation paid on the application; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (ss 70, 71)` 5000 `the last day`
        OTHERWISE `if convicted, a fine of up to the amount; a prosecution must be commenced by the last day (s 71)` 5000 `the last day`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `the date` 2 `years after` `the date of the alleged offence`
